WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.602
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented actress

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:03.936
you know from shows like
"Brockmire" and "Dirty John."

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:06.639
She created and wrote
the new series "The Chair,"

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.775
which is streaming now
on Netflix.

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:10.476
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.476 --> 00:00:11.978
-You look at
your evaluations yet?

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.279
-Yes.

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:16.449
-And what did they say?

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:18.818
-That I'm too exciting.

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:23.923
Wait a minute.
Was he always in here?

00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:25.524
-Oh, I haven't had time
to redecorate.

00:00:25.524 --> 00:00:29.929
-I gave him a hand job
in his car when I got tenure,

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:31.263
to celebrate.

00:00:31.263 --> 00:00:33.933
-That's the president
of the college from like 1924.

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:36.202
-Ooh, looks like the guy
I gave a hand job to.

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:39.005
-Also, how is that
a celebration for you?

00:00:39.005 --> 00:00:40.306
-Good point.

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:43.176
He owes me an orgasm,
whoever he is.

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:45.611
-Please welcome back to the show
and the studio

00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:47.280
Amanda Peet, everybody.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.718
♪♪

00:00:52.718 --> 00:00:55.421
-Ow.

00:00:55.421 --> 00:00:56.455
-Hi.
-Hi.

00:00:56.455 --> 00:00:57.490
-Lovely to see you.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.625
-Lovely to see you.
-Come on up!

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:01.861
-Hi, everybody!

00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:03.729
[ Laughs ]

00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:06.165
-It's a little strange.
You get used to it really fast.

00:01:06.165 --> 00:01:08.101
-Okay.
-But I --

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:09.435
I notice something's
a little different

00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:11.204
since the last time I saw you.

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:13.172
You're all splinted up.
What happened?

00:01:13.172 --> 00:01:15.408
-So, I had an operation
yesterday.

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:17.076
-Okay.
-On --

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:18.778
I had -- I had tennis elbow.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:19.979
-Okay, from playing tennis.

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:21.881
-From playing pickleball.
-From playing pickleball.

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:23.149
-Yeah.

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:25.885
-Did you say it was tennis
when the doctor asked?

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:26.919
-No, I --

00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:28.187
-You copped to it
being pickleball?

00:01:28.187 --> 00:01:29.955
-I copped to it.
But I'm realizing

00:01:29.955 --> 00:01:32.258
now that I'm on this show,

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:34.994
and I spent this money on this
half sweater --

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.062
-Yeah.

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:37.229
-...that --

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:39.766
[ Laughs ]

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:42.401
I went to a store, 'cause, like,
my dress wouldn't --

00:01:42.401 --> 00:01:45.672
The dress that I picked to come
here didn't go over the thing.

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:47.440
-You didn't think ahead to that.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.975
-Well, I thought I had,

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:51.144
but I didn't realize
how huge it was going to be.

00:01:51.144 --> 00:01:53.246
So then I walked into the store,

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:55.748
and the nice gentleman
showed me this.

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:58.017
[ Laughs ]
-So he -- Did he know?

00:01:58.017 --> 00:02:00.786
Did they have that in, like,
the pickle elbow section?

00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:02.455
-Yes. The pickle elbow section?

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:03.956
I'm wearing it backwards, too,

00:02:03.956 --> 00:02:05.391
'cause the hole was
on the wrong side.

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:07.326
-Oh, so it is --
So you didn't cut off an arm?

00:02:07.326 --> 00:02:10.096
That is an actual --
Well, I don't know!

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:11.597
They really had --

00:02:11.597 --> 00:02:13.866
Well, it just seems amazing to
me that you'd walk into a store

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:15.935
and the guy would be like,
"We got exactly --"

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:17.469
-That's why it's crazy.

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:18.804
-That's amazing.
-Yeah.

00:02:18.804 --> 00:02:20.373
"Only in New York, baby."

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.841
-Yeah. "We got sweaters
for no arms.

00:02:22.841 --> 00:02:24.544
We got sweaters for three arms.

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:26.813
It's New York City.
What do you want?"

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:29.148
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.148 --> 00:02:30.783
-Oh, my God.
I love these people.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.618
-Yeah, they're really nice.

00:02:32.618 --> 00:02:35.722
You think, like, it couldn't
be worse, but it could.

00:02:35.722 --> 00:02:37.690
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:39.959
Imagine if they weren't here.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:42.294
-Yeah. So, the point
of what I'm saying is,

00:02:42.294 --> 00:02:45.097
I'm not good enough

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:49.468
to warrant all of this --
this -- this operation.

00:02:49.468 --> 00:02:51.137
-Good enough at pickleball?
-Pickleball.

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:53.206
-Yeah.
-And this whole discussion

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:54.474
that we're having right now.

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:56.642
-But you must play it enough
to put wear and tear

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:57.943
on the elbow, right?
-Yes.

00:02:57.943 --> 00:03:02.014
Like, it's a very bad
combination of cockiness...

00:03:02.014 --> 00:03:03.515
-Yeah.
-...and old age.

00:03:03.515 --> 00:03:04.784
-Oh, so now that's interesting,

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:06.653
cause I do think, right,
like, as you get older

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:08.087
and this happens to me
with sports, too,

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:10.923
like, what it takes to, like,
make you injured goes down.

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:12.324
So how often --

00:03:12.324 --> 00:03:13.692
-I literally
waved to the surgeon,

00:03:13.692 --> 00:03:15.694
and I said, "See you in --
See you next year."

00:03:15.694 --> 00:03:17.229
-Yeah.
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:17.229 --> 00:03:19.164
-How often would you say
you played --

00:03:19.164 --> 00:03:21.534
Is this once a week
you're playing pickleball?

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:23.870
-I did -- I started playing
about three times a week.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:24.971
I became obsessed.

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:26.338
-Okay, gotcha.
-I did.

00:03:26.338 --> 00:03:27.840
-Do you play with the same
people all of the time?

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.442
-Yes.
-And how are they doing

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:30.977
physically?
-They're fine.

00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:32.345
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:34.113
-Are they the same age?

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:35.614
-Yes!
-Okay.

00:03:35.614 --> 00:03:37.383
-But one of them
was a star tennis player.

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:40.119
-Gotcha. Do you think
this was a technique issue?

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:44.123
Like, do you think if you got
some pickleball instruction,

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:46.259
you could maybe put
less wear on the elbow?

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:48.727
-Apparently it's
to do with old age.

00:03:48.727 --> 00:03:50.930
It's, like, something to do
with having a weak shoulder,

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.165
so then you overcompensate
by using your --

00:03:53.165 --> 00:03:54.500
-Gotcha.
-Your --

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.002
Is anybody still awake?

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:58.971
-Was it -- Was it
the most embarrassing

00:03:58.971 --> 00:04:00.974
when you told
the people you played with,

00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:02.808
because they know exactly
how good you are?

00:04:02.808 --> 00:04:05.311
-It was most embarrassing
when I went to the surgeon,

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:06.845
and he brought back
the pictures.

00:04:06.845 --> 00:04:08.747
-[ Laughs ] Okay, gotcha.

00:04:08.747 --> 00:04:11.217
Was it just pictures
of people playing pickleball?

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:13.952
-'Cause he does people -- He
does, like, the Mets and stuff.

00:04:13.952 --> 00:04:16.155
-Gotcha.
-And so I was like,

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:17.623
"I know your rate of success

00:04:17.623 --> 00:04:20.126
with 20-year-olds
who are genius athletes.

00:04:20.126 --> 00:04:22.428
I need to know
you can fix an old lady."

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:25.197
-Yeah. "And I want you to know,
you can fix this old lady

00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:27.333
who will never do
any of the rehab, and intend --

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:28.801
I intend --"
Are you going to do the rehab?

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:31.070
-I have to do the rehab!
The rehab is everything.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.506
-Are you that kind of person
that is actually going to do it?

00:04:33.506 --> 00:04:35.408
-Well, otherwise,
I feel like this is --

00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:37.777
He just did this part.
Now I have to do the rest.

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:39.812
-Oh, my gosh. Well,
I wish you the best of luck.

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:41.547
We really want to see you
back out there.

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:43.015
-That's why I bought
this sweater.

00:04:43.015 --> 00:04:44.584
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:46.752
-Hey, congratulations
on this show.

00:04:46.752 --> 00:04:49.555
This is a wonderful show
that you co-wrote.

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:51.290
-Thank you.
-And it's about --

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:53.092
You have Sandra Oh,
first of all.

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:54.660
-Yeah.
-And you got Sandra Oh

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.228
through your friend
Sarah Paulson, yeah?

00:04:56.228 --> 00:04:59.699
-Yes. She made some calls
for me, as she sometimes does.

00:04:59.699 --> 00:05:02.001
-Okay. And so, had you known
Sandra Oh before --

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:03.802
'Cause she seems so perfect
for the part.

00:05:03.802 --> 00:05:06.005
-Yes, we had met
a couple of times.

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:08.374
I knew her peripherally, but
I didn't have her phone number.

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:09.675
-Okay, so you got
her phone number.

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:12.512
-So -- No, no.
I just had Sarah do it.

00:05:12.512 --> 00:05:15.114
-You had Sarah call and be like,
"Do you want to do this show?"

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:17.783
-Or she DM'd --
What do you people do who are --

00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:19.651
-We are exactly the same age.

00:05:19.651 --> 00:05:22.221
Don't try to --

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:24.757
My doctor told me
I can't even play pickleball.

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:26.625
That's my age.
-Really?

00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:28.727
-He's like,
"It'll go right away."

00:05:28.727 --> 00:05:30.162
-It'll go right away.
-No, not really.

00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:31.630
-"This will go right away."
-Yeah, as like everything --

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:33.165
-Well, I thought maybe you had
an injury.

00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:35.301
-You -- But you got so --
-'Cause there's a lot of torque,

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:36.535
you know, and it's --
-So she DM'd?

00:05:36.535 --> 00:05:38.471
-Okay. Yeah.
I'll go back to that.

00:05:38.471 --> 00:05:39.672
She DM'd --

00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:41.507
-I just feel like
I would like to go back,

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.108
'cause I feel like we've already
put a lot of time

00:05:43.108 --> 00:05:45.077
into pickleball.
-I know, and it's not,

00:05:45.077 --> 00:05:47.246
like I said,
commensurate with --

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:48.714
-Yeah.

00:05:48.714 --> 00:05:50.215
-Yeah. Okay.

00:05:50.215 --> 00:05:52.151
Paulson is my dear friend.

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:54.787
-Mm-hmm.
-And she also sort of acts like

00:05:54.787 --> 00:05:58.390
my casting director/agent/Jewish
grandmother.

00:05:58.390 --> 00:05:59.825
-Oh, that's a nice
combo of things.

00:05:59.825 --> 00:06:01.327
-Yeah, yeah.
-And then I --

00:06:01.327 --> 00:06:03.195
Well, you know, I couldn't help
but notice Holland Taylor,

00:06:03.195 --> 00:06:05.898
her partner, is in this,
as well, in the clip, no less.

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.266
-Yes.
-And so how did --

00:06:07.266 --> 00:06:09.601
She help you do that, as well?
-Of course she did.

00:06:09.601 --> 00:06:11.437
-Wow. So Sarah Paulson
should really --

00:06:11.437 --> 00:06:13.839
Did she get
a special thanks in this?

00:06:13.839 --> 00:06:15.508
-She --

00:06:15.508 --> 00:06:17.309
Thank you, Bird.

00:06:17.309 --> 00:06:18.711
I'm thanking her here and now.

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:19.879
-There's a real coup --

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:21.847
There's a real casting coup
in this show.

00:06:21.847 --> 00:06:25.518
'Cause it's about an English
department at a college,

00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:26.919
at a fictional college,
it should be noted,

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:29.121
but beautifully shot
at Carnegie Mellon?

00:06:29.121 --> 00:06:31.990
-We were at Chatham.
-Chatham.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:34.994
And here's your friend, I guess
I don't know if you know him,

00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:38.463
but David Duchovny shows up
as a version of himself,

00:06:38.463 --> 00:06:40.500
in that Speedo.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:44.470
-Well, so, you know he wore
a red Speedo in the "X-Files."

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:45.871
-Yeah.
-And I asked him --

00:06:45.871 --> 00:06:48.474
begged him to do this part,
but then I thought of this idea,

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:51.310
"What if he was
in the red Speedo?"

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.512
But then I realized I had
to make that phone call to him.

00:06:53.512 --> 00:06:55.814
-So he'd already said yes, but
he didn't know the Speedo part.

00:06:55.814 --> 00:06:57.116
-Yeah, well,
I hadn't thought of it yet.

00:06:57.116 --> 00:06:59.384
-Yeah. That's your story.
-So then I practiced

00:06:59.384 --> 00:07:02.354
my phone call --
-Yeah.

00:07:02.354 --> 00:07:04.022
-I practiced
my phone call to him,

00:07:04.022 --> 00:07:07.593
but I really just felt like
a sleazy director from the '70s.

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:10.329
-Right. You mean that you got,
like, the young ingenue

00:07:10.329 --> 00:07:12.264
to commit.
-Yeah, and then I was going

00:07:12.264 --> 00:07:13.933
to be like, "But the thing --
So the thing is, is like

00:07:13.933 --> 00:07:16.034
you need to just
wear your skivvies."

00:07:16.034 --> 00:07:18.104
-Yeah.
-But he said yes right away.

00:07:18.104 --> 00:07:19.672
Because, I mean,
look at his body.

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:22.141
-It's pretty impressive.
I mean, I'm not going to lie.

00:07:22.141 --> 00:07:24.910
I bet he was in that
when you called.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:26.211
-He was waiting by the phone.

00:07:26.211 --> 00:07:28.748
He was just waiting
for somebody to be like --

00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:30.082
[ Laughter ]

00:07:30.082 --> 00:07:32.084
-He must have been so happy.

00:07:32.084 --> 00:07:33.585
-I was, like,
"Is this uncomfortable?"

00:07:33.585 --> 00:07:37.289
He was like, "No, no.
Where do I need to be? What?"

00:07:37.289 --> 00:07:40.426
-You -- Your husband, David
Benioff, is a producer on this,

00:07:40.426 --> 00:07:42.829
but this is your rodeo.
This is your show.

00:07:42.829 --> 00:07:47.466
He -- You know, he was a writer,
creator of "Game of Thrones,"

00:07:47.466 --> 00:07:49.434
and we've talked about the fact

00:07:49.434 --> 00:07:51.570
that you kind of had to bear
the burden of being a parent

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:52.805
for those many years.

00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:54.973
Was he --
How did he do in his role?

00:07:54.973 --> 00:07:57.944
-He was amazing. And, you know,
I was in Pittsburgh.

00:07:57.944 --> 00:07:58.978
-Yeah.

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:01.713
-And show running is intense.

00:08:01.713 --> 00:08:02.748
-It's very intense.

00:08:02.748 --> 00:08:05.284
-And so, he had all three kids.

00:08:05.284 --> 00:08:08.621
And a couple of times, he --

00:08:08.621 --> 00:08:11.023
He had, you know,
a Zoom conference,

00:08:11.023 --> 00:08:13.892
parent-teacher conference
or something would happen,

00:08:13.892 --> 00:08:16.962
and he would just
start complaining

00:08:16.962 --> 00:08:19.030
that he -- that it was too much,
he was on his own,

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:22.201
and I would just be like
[Stammers]

00:08:22.201 --> 00:08:23.302
Nine years.

00:08:23.302 --> 00:08:25.604
-Yeah.
-Nine years...

00:08:25.604 --> 00:08:27.306
-Yeah.
-...you were in Belfast.

00:08:27.306 --> 00:08:29.208
-Right. Yeah, not even
Pittsburgh.

00:08:29.208 --> 00:08:30.910
He was in Belfast.

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:32.945
Croatia, a lot of time in
Croatia, right?

00:08:32.945 --> 00:08:34.380
-Yeah, that, too.
-Yeah.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.482
-But, boy, was that fun.
-Yeah, that must have been nice.

00:08:36.482 --> 00:08:38.283
-Boy, was that fun in my
marriage to just be like --

00:08:38.283 --> 00:08:41.453
-Also, it must have been --
'Cause we've done this, as well,

00:08:41.453 --> 00:08:44.357
where, you know, as a parent
you do a lot of Zoom things now,

00:08:44.357 --> 00:08:46.025
and we complain about them,

00:08:46.025 --> 00:08:49.795
forgetting that in regular
times, you'd have to go places.

00:08:49.795 --> 00:08:51.630
-I was at those things.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:54.533
-Yeah.
-With the other parents, alone,

00:08:54.533 --> 00:08:57.303
schmoozing and ooh-ing
and ahh-ing.

00:08:57.303 --> 00:08:58.404
-And other parents were like,

00:08:58.404 --> 00:08:59.605
"Should we ask her
to play pickleball?"

00:08:59.605 --> 00:09:02.007
And they were like,
"She's too old."

00:09:02.007 --> 00:09:04.377
"She'll fall apart."

00:09:04.377 --> 00:09:06.078
I can't believe you found
a one-armed sweater.

00:09:06.078 --> 00:09:09.048
-I found a one-armed sweater!

00:09:09.048 --> 00:09:10.816
It's, like,
the greatest thing ever.

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:14.453
-I want to close
by saying the show is fantastic,

00:09:14.453 --> 00:09:16.255
and everybody
should check it out.

00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:17.956
You guys, that is Amanda Peet.

00:09:17.956 --> 00:09:19.659
"The Chair"
is available to stream

00:09:19.659 --> 00:09:20.693
in its entirety on Netflix.

